In this step, we will explore packaging and launching our Spring Boot container on AWS Fargate (Amazon ECS Serverless compute engine) alongside an AWS X-Ray daemon sidecar, and configuring an internal Application Load Balancer (ALB) for private traffic routing.
1. ECS Fargate and Internal ALB Architecture The application runs entirely within the Private Subnet with Egress provisioned in step 4:
Amazon ECS Cluster (fcaj-products-cluster): Logical grouping managing container services and tasks. Fargate Task Definition (ProductsTaskDef): Specifies container resource allocation and behaviors: CPU: 512 units (0.5 vCPU), Memory: 1024 MiB (1 GB). Primary Container (web): Runs the Spring Boot app pulled from ECR fcaj-products-service:1.0.0, listens on port 8080, and streams logs to CloudWatch. Sidecar Container (xray-daemon): Runs public.ecr.aws/xray/aws-xray-daemon:latest, listens on UDP port 2000, and relays application trace data to AWS X-Ray. Task Role: Grants DynamoDB read/write permissions and attaches the AWSXRayDaemonWriteAccess policy. ECS Fargate Service (fcaj-products-service): Maintains desired task count (desiredCount: 1), with automatic recovery enabled by the deployment circuit breaker. Tasks run without public IPs for maximum security. Internal Application Load Balancer (InternalAlb): Placed within Private Subnets, listening on port 80 and forwarding requests to container port 8080 guided by the health check at /actuator/health. 2. Write the ECS Stack Definition (ecs-stack.ts) Navigate to IaC-ECS-CDK/cdk/lib and open ecs-stack.ts (or create it). We will construct each component of the Stack step by step below:
2.1. Imports and Props Interface First, import the required AWS CDK constructs and define the EcsStackProps interface to accept dependency inputs (vpc, table, repository):
import * as cdk from &#39;aws-cdk-lib&#39;; import { Construct } from &#39;constructs&#39;; import * as ec2 from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-ec2&#39;; import * as ecs from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs&#39;; import * as elbv2 from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-elasticloadbalancingv2&#39;; import * as ecr from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecr&#39;; import * as iam from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-iam&#39;; import * as dynamodb from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-dynamodb&#39;; interface EcsStackProps extends cdk.StackProps { vpc: ec2.IVpc; table: dynamodb.ITable; repository: ecr.IRepository; } export class EcsStack extends cdk.Stack { public readonly alb: elbv2.ApplicationLoadBalancer; public readonly albListener: elbv2.ApplicationListener; public readonly fargateService: ecs.FargateService; constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: EcsStackProps) { super(scope, id, props); 2.2. Initialize Amazon ECS Cluster Create the ECS Cluster attached to our private VPC:
// 1. Initialize ECS Cluster within the VPC const cluster = new ecs.Cluster(this, &#39;ProductsServiceCluster&#39;, { clusterName: &#39;fcaj-products-cluster&#39;, vpc: props.vpc, }); 2.3. Define Fargate Task Definition and Task Role Permissions Allocate container resources (CPU 512, RAM 1024 MiB) and grant the Task Role permissions to read/write DynamoDB and stream traces to AWS X-Ray:
// 2. Define Fargate Task Definition (CPU 512, RAM 1024 MiB) const taskDefinition = new ecs.FargateTaskDefinition(this, &#39;ProductsTaskDef&#39;, { cpu: 512, memoryLimitMiB: 1024, }); // Grant DynamoDB and X-Ray permissions to Task Role props.table.grantReadWriteData(taskDefinition.taskRole); taskDefinition.taskRole.addManagedPolicy( iam.ManagedPolicy.fromAwsManagedPolicyName(&#39;AWSXRayDaemonWriteAccess&#39;) ); 2.4. Add Spring Boot Container and X-Ray Daemon Sidecar Configure two containers running concurrently within the same Task:
Primary web container: Pulls image from ECR (tag 1.0.0), maps port 8080, and configures CloudWatch logging. Sidecar xray-daemon: Collects trace data over UDP port 2000. // 3. Add primary Spring Boot Web Container const appContainer = taskDefinition.addContainer(&#39;web&#39;, { image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromEcrRepository(props.repository, &#39;1.0.0&#39;), logging: new ecs.AwsLogDriver({ streamPrefix: &#39;products-service&#39; }), environment: { SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: &#39;default&#39;, AWS_REGION: this.region, AWS_XRAY_DAEMON_ADDRESS: &#39;127.0.0.1:2000&#39;, }, }); appContainer.addPortMappings({ containerPort: 8080 }); // 4. Add AWS X-Ray Daemon Sidecar Container const xrayContainer = taskDefinition.addContainer(&#39;xray-daemon&#39;, { image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromRegistry(&#39;public.ecr.aws/xray/aws-xray-daemon:latest&#39;), cpu: 32, memoryLimitMiB: 256, logging: new ecs.AwsLogDriver({ streamPrefix: &#39;xray&#39; }), }); xrayContainer.addPortMappings({ containerPort: 2000, protocol: ecs.Protocol.UDP }); 2.5. Initialize ECS Fargate Service in Private Subnets Instantiate the service with desiredCount: 1, hosted in PRIVATE_WITH_EGRESS subnets with automated rollback on deployment failure:
// 5. Initialize ECS Fargate Service in Private Subnets this.fargateService = new ecs.FargateService(this, &#39;ProductsFargateService&#39;, { cluster, taskDefinition, serviceName: &#39;fcaj-products-service&#39;, desiredCount: 1, assignPublicIp: false, circuitBreaker: { rollback: true, }, minHealthyPercent: 100, maxHealthyPercent: 200, vpcSubnets: { subnetType: ec2.SubnetType.PRIVATE_WITH_EGRESS, }, }); 2.6. Initialize Internal ALB and Configure Health Checks Provision the internal Application Load Balancer (internetFacing: false), establish listener port 80, and route traffic to service port 8080 verified by health checks at /actuator/health:
// 6. Initialize Internal Application Load Balancer this.alb = new elbv2.ApplicationLoadBalancer(this, &#39;InternalAlb&#39;, { vpc: props.vpc, internetFacing: false, vpcSubnets: { subnetType: ec2.SubnetType.PRIVATE_WITH_EGRESS, }, }); this.albListener = this.alb.addListener(&#39;AlbListener&#39;, { port: 80, open: true, }); // Forward traffic to ECS Tasks with Health Check configuration this.albListener.addTargets(&#39;EcsTargets&#39;, { port: 8080, protocol: elbv2.ApplicationProtocol.HTTP, targets: [this.fargateService], healthCheck: { path: &#39;/actuator/health&#39;, healthyHttpCodes: &#39;200&#39;, interval: cdk.Duration.seconds(30), }, }); } } 2.7. Complete Source Code of ecs-stack.ts Bringing all pieces together, the full ecs-stack.ts file is structured as follows:
import * as cdk from &#39;aws-cdk-lib&#39;; import { Construct } from &#39;constructs&#39;; import * as ec2 from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-ec2&#39;; import * as ecs from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs&#39;; import * as elbv2 from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-elasticloadbalancingv2&#39;; import * as ecr from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecr&#39;; import * as iam from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-iam&#39;; import * as dynamodb from &#39;aws-cdk-lib/aws-dynamodb&#39;; interface EcsStackProps extends cdk.StackProps { vpc: ec2.IVpc; table: dynamodb.ITable; repository: ecr.IRepository; } export class EcsStack extends cdk.Stack { public readonly alb: elbv2.ApplicationLoadBalancer; public readonly albListener: elbv2.ApplicationListener; public readonly fargateService: ecs.FargateService; constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: EcsStackProps) { super(scope, id, props); const cluster = new ecs.Cluster(this, &#39;ProductsServiceCluster&#39;, { clusterName: &#39;fcaj-products-cluster&#39;, vpc: props.vpc, }); const taskDefinition = new ecs.FargateTaskDefinition(this, &#39;ProductsTaskDef&#39;, { cpu: 512, memoryLimitMiB: 1024, }); props.table.grantReadWriteData(taskDefinition.taskRole); taskDefinition.taskRole.addManagedPolicy( iam.ManagedPolicy.fromAwsManagedPolicyName(&#39;AWSXRayDaemonWriteAccess&#39;) ); const appContainer = taskDefinition.addContainer(&#39;web&#39;, { image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromEcrRepository(props.repository, &#39;1.0.0&#39;), logging: new ecs.AwsLogDriver({ streamPrefix: &#39;products-service&#39; }), environment: { SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: &#39;default&#39;, AWS_REGION: this.region, AWS_XRAY_DAEMON_ADDRESS: &#39;127.0.0.1:2000&#39;, }, }); appContainer.addPortMappings({ containerPort: 8080 }); const xrayContainer = taskDefinition.addContainer(&#39;xray-daemon&#39;, { image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromRegistry(&#39;public.ecr.aws/xray/aws-xray-daemon:latest&#39;), cpu: 32, memoryLimitMiB: 256, logging: new ecs.AwsLogDriver({ streamPrefix: &#39;xray&#39; }), }); xrayContainer.addPortMappings({ containerPort: 2000, protocol: ecs.Protocol.UDP }); this.fargateService = new ecs.FargateService(this, &#39;ProductsFargateService&#39;, { cluster, taskDefinition, serviceName: &#39;fcaj-products-service&#39;, desiredCount: 1, assignPublicIp: false, circuitBreaker: { rollback: true, }, minHealthyPercent: 100, maxHealthyPercent: 200, vpcSubnets: { subnetType: ec2.SubnetType.PRIVATE_WITH_EGRESS, }, }); this.alb = new elbv2.ApplicationLoadBalancer(this, &#39;InternalAlb&#39;, { vpc: props.vpc, internetFacing: false, vpcSubnets: { subnetType: ec2.SubnetType.PRIVATE_WITH_EGRESS, }, }); this.albListener = this.alb.addListener(&#39;AlbListener&#39;, { port: 80, open: true, }); this.albListener.addTargets(&#39;EcsTargets&#39;, { port: 8080, protocol: elbv2.ApplicationProtocol.HTTP, targets: [this.fargateService], healthCheck: { path: &#39;/actuator/health&#39;, healthyHttpCodes: &#39;200&#39;, interval: cdk.Duration.seconds(30), }, }); } } 3. Key Properties Explanation assignPublicIp: false: Guarantees containers receive no public IP addresses, eliminating direct exposure to the public Internet. circuitBreaker: { rollback: true }: Automatically detects failed task deployments (such as container crash-loops or failing health checks) and rolls back to the previous stable task definition revision. healthCheck: { path: &lsquo;/actuator/health&rsquo; }: The load balancer periodically executes HTTP GET requests to the Spring Boot Actuator endpoint every 30 seconds. Tasks reporting 200 OK are marked as Healthy to receive traffic. public readonly albListener: Exposes the listener object so it can be passed to the Network Load Balancer (NLB) stack in the next step to complete the VPC Link pipeline.
